\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Models and Panel Construction}

We select 15 LLM judges spanning 6 provider families and 3 capability tiers, listed in Table~\ref{tab:models}. Nine models are accessed via the OpenRouter API;\footnote{All API models were accessed via OpenRouter between March--April 2026. Model snapshots may have changed after this period.} six open-weight models are served locally using vLLM~0.21.0 with Activation-aware Weight Quantization (AWQ) INT4 on an NVIDIA RTX PRO~6000 Blackwell (96\,GB). API models are assigned to \emph{frontier}, \emph{mid-tier}, or \emph{lightweight} tiers following the provider capability classification; all six local models fall into the lightweight tier, as INT4 quantization substantially reduces judge accuracy on the pairwise judgment task (Table~\ref{tab:models}). We study three panel sizes $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$ (all odd, majority-vote threshold $t = \lceil K/2 \rceil$). Exhaustive enumeration yields $\binom{15}{3} = 455$ panels at $K{=}3$, $\binom{15}{5} = 3{,}003$ at $K{=}5$, and $\binom{15}{7} = 6{,}435$ at $K{=}7$, covering every combination of families and tiers. Each panel evaluates all benchmark items under all attack types, producing a complete panel $\times$ item $\times$ attack tensor. The primary analysis uses $K{=}3$; \S\ref{sec:cross_k} extends to larger panels.

\subsection{Benchmarks and Attack Types}

Two benchmarks test distinct evaluation capabilities: MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}, a knowledge-intensive multiple-choice benchmark spanning 57 subjects, and ARC \citep{clark2018arc}, a reasoning-intensive science question benchmark. We sample 300 items from each. All models use temperature~0 for deterministic outputs (temperature has negligible effect on $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$; Appendix~\ref{app:temperature}).

Each benchmark is paired with six attack types: (1)~\textbf{prompt injection}, adversarial instructions overriding the evaluation prompt \citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023not}; (2)~\textbf{sycophancy}, flattering language exploiting judge preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; (3)~\textbf{score manipulation}, explicit numerical cues anchoring scoring; (4)~\textbf{verbosity bias}, padding with irrelevant detail \citep{zheng2023judging}; (5)~\textbf{position bias}, reordering to exploit positional preferences \citep{wang2023fair}; and (6)~\textbf{authority bias}, fabricated credentials inflating credibility. The 2 benchmarks $\times$ 6 attacks produce 12 conditions, each evaluated across all 455 panels (templates and implementation details in Appendix~\ref{app:attacks}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Bench.} & \textbf{Attack} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ & $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ & \textbf{Dom.} \\
\midrule
MMLU & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Score manip.   & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
MMLU & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Position bias  & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
MMLU & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\addlinespace
ARC  & Prompt inj.    & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Sycophancy     & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Score manip.   & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Verbosity      & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
ARC  & Position bias  & n.s.    & $+^{*}$ & $K$ \\
ARC  & Authority bias & $+^{*}$ & $+^{*}$ & Co \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Standardized regression coefficients for $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ across 12 conditions (455 panels, log-log OLS). $+^{*}$/$-^{*}$: FDR-significant positive/negative (Benjamini--Hochberg, $q < 0.05$); n.s.: not significant. Dom.\ column: $\eta$ = $\eta_{\max}$ sole dominant, $K$ = $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ sole dominant, Co = co-dominant \citep{budescu1993dominance}.}
\label{tab:dominance}
\end{table}

\subsection{Vulnerability Metrics}

Per-judge vulnerability under attack $a$ is the fraction of items where judge $j$ is fooled: $\eta_j(a) = \Pr(X_j^a {=} 1)$, estimated empirically over the item set. The weakest-link vulnerability $\eta_{\max}(a) = \max_{j \in \mathcal{P}} \eta_j(a)$ captures the most susceptible member of each panel. The panel attack success rate $\mathrm{ASR}(\mathcal{P}, a)$ is the fraction of items where at least $t$ judges are simultaneously fooled. Panel diversity is quantified by $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (Equation~\ref{eq:keff}), computed from pairwise MI between judges' three-category winner labels (model~A wins, model~B wins, tie) on clean data, using the plug-in estimator with Miller--Madow bias correction. The $\exp(-2\,\mathrm{MI}(i,j))$ term measures pairwise alienation: 1 when independent, 0 under full dependence. Under a Gaussian copula this yields $K_{\mathrm{eff}} = K - \frac{1}{K}\sum_{i}\sum_{j \neq i} \rho_{ij}^2$ \citep{nelsen2006copulas}; goodness-of-fit rejects the Gaussian copula for 97--100\% of pairs, yet plug-in MI and closed-form rankings agree closely ($\rho{\approx}0.86$; Appendix~\ref{app:copula_gof}). As an alternative diversity measure, $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} is defined as $1 - \bar{\kappa}$, where $\bar{\kappa}$ is the mean pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$ on clean winner labels. Together, $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link channel) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (diversity channel) serve as the two predictors in the regression framework below.

\subsection{Aggregation Methods}

Beyond majority vote ($\geq \lceil K/2 \rceil$ approvals), we evaluate three alternatives: \emph{accuracy-weighted vote} ($w_j \propto \mathrm{acc}_j$; accept if $\sum_j w_j \cdot \mathbf{1}[J_j \text{ approves}] > 0.5$), \emph{threshold vote} (unanimity, $t{=}K$), and \emph{Bayesian calibration} ($P(\text{correct} \mid \mathbf{v}) \propto P(\text{correct}) \prod_j P(v_j \mid \text{correct})$; accept if posterior $> 0.5$).

\subsection{Statistical Framework}
\label{sec:stat_framework}

To disentangle the contributions of panel diversity and individual judge robustness, we fit a log-log OLS regression for each of the 12 conditions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:regression}
\log(\mathrm{ASR} + \epsilon) = \alpha + \beta_1 \log(K_{\mathrm{eff}}) + \beta_2 \log(\eta_{\max} + \epsilon),
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ to handle zero values (sensitivity to $\epsilon$ is analyzed in Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon}). Standardized coefficients $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}} = \hat{\beta} \cdot \mathrm{SD}(\log x) / \mathrm{SD}(\log y)$ enable direct comparison of effect magnitudes across predictors and conditions. We apply Benjamini--Hochberg (BH) correction for multiple testing, controlling the false discovery rate (FDR) per predictor across the 12 conditions.

Dominance analysis \citep{budescu1993dominance} decomposes $R^2$ into each predictor's average incremental contribution across all subsets. Generalized additive models (GAMs) with smooth terms test whether $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s effect direction varies between targeted attacks (prompt injection, score manipulation, position bias) and shared-bias attacks (sycophancy, verbosity, authority bias).

\subsection{Robustness to Non-Independence}
\label{sec:robustness_method}

The 455 panels share judges combinatorially (each appears in 91 panels), inducing correlated residuals. We apply three corrections \citep[see][for a practitioner's guide]{cameron2015practitioners}: clustered standard errors (SE; CR1, 3-position max-SE), mixed-effects models (random judge intercepts), and wild cluster bootstrap ($B{=}9{,}999$, Rademacher weights, 3-position max-$p$) following \citet{cameron2008bootstrap}. Cluster counts (13, ${\leq}11$, ${\leq}9$ at $K{=}3,5,7$) fall below the ${\geq}20$ threshold; we use Rademacher weights following \citet{mackinnon2018wild}. Fractional logit \citep{papke1996econometric} with clustered SE serves as an $\epsilon$-free robustness check (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness_methods}). Monte Carlo calibration (1{,}000 permutations, $B{=}999$) confirms the wild cluster bootstrap does not over-reject in any of 36 conditions (max null rejection rate ${=}1.6\%$ at nominal $\alpha{=}5\%$), establishing reported significance counts as conservative lower bounds (Appendix~\ref{app:monte_carlo}).

